@* LoginLayout.razor — Phase 0.11 (redesigned Launch-D5 — split-screen)
   50/50 split-screen layout with TakLight theme colors:
   - LEFT panel (cream background): the login form, centered.
     The form is rendered by Login.razor (a child of this layout via @Body).
     Above the form: a localized heading + subtext.
   - RIGHT panel (deep forest green with radial gradient glow):
     Brand mark + title + subtitle + intro paragraph + 4 gold-dot
     feature bullets + copyright footer.
     The language switcher is a gold rounded-square button in the
     top-right corner — clicking it toggles between EN and FA.

   CRITICAL — STATIC SSR (no Blazor circuit):
   Login.razor and ChangePassword.razor are STATIC-SSR pages (no
   Blazor circuit). Blazor's @onclick handler requires a live circuit
   to round-trip the click event to the server — with no circuit,
   @onclick silently never fires. The language switcher therefore uses
   plain HTML onclick + inline JS (takSwitchCulture at the bottom of
   this file), exactly as the previous version did. The password eye-
   toggle on Login.razor uses the same pattern (onclick="takTogglePw"
   — see Login.razor's inline <script>).

   CRITICAL — RTL Persian text input bidi fix PRESERVED:
   The form's native <input> elements have dir="ltr" hardcoded so that
   typing "miow2323!" in Persian mode doesn't show as "!miow2323"
   (the bidi algorithm places neutral characters at the start of the
   LTR run when the surrounding context is RTL). This fix lives in
   Login.razor itself (NOT in this layout) — the dir="ltr" attribute
   is on the <input> elements. The new layout does NOT touch this; it
   only changes the LOOKS (split-screen shell + TakLight colors), not
   the form internals.

   TAKLIGHT THEME COLORS:
   All colors use var(--tm-*) tokens defined by tak-light.css (loaded
   by RadzenTheme + theme.js). When the user switches to dark theme
   (tak-dark.css), the same variables resolve to dark-theme values —
   but this layout is the LOGIN page, which is shown BEFORE the user
   is authenticated. The login page uses TakLight colors REGARDLESS
   of the user's saved theme preference (the in-app theme switcher
   only affects the post-login pages). This is intentional: the login
   page is the brand storefront, and the brand is TakLight. *@

@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@inject IStringLocalizer<LoginLayout> Localizer
@using System.Globalization
@using TakOne.WebUI.Services

<div class="login-shell">
    @* ── LEFT panel — form side (cream background) ── *@
    <section class="login-panel login-panel--form" aria-label="@Localizer["FormHeading"]">
        <header class="login-form-header">
            <h1 class="login-form-title">@Localizer["FormHeading"]</h1>
            <p class="login-form-subtext">@Localizer["FormSubtext"]</p>
        </header>

        <div class="login-form-body">
            @Body
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ── RIGHT panel — branding side (deep green with radial glow) ── *@
    <aside class="login-panel login-panel--brand" aria-label="TakOne brand">
        @* Radial glow — a subtle spotlight effect behind the brand
           text + icon area, like the design mockup. *@
        <div class="login-brand-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>

        @* Language switcher — top-right corner. JUST the text label
           ("EN" / "فا"), no logo. The TakLogo.png image is moved INTO
           the .login-brand-content stack below (above the title) per
           the user directive: "the logo should be on top of that (as
           of picture) not on top of the green page as it is now".
           The logo now sits at the top of the brand content stack,
           above the "Alone at the peak," / "تنها در اوج،" title. *@
        <button type="button"
                class="login-lang-switch"
                aria-label="@Localizer["LangSwitchAriaLabel"]"
                title="@Localizer["LangSwitchAriaLabel"]"
                onclick="takSwitchCulture('@(CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture.Name == "fa-IR" ? "en-US" : "fa-IR")')">
            @(CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture.Name == "fa-IR" ? "EN" : "فا")
        </button>

        <div class="login-brand-content">
            @* Brand logo — at the TOP of the brand content stack,
               above the title. Previously this was in the top-right
               corner; now it's part of the vertical content flow. *@
            <img src="TakLogo.png"
                 alt="TakOne"
                 class="login-brand-content-logo" />

            <h2 class="login-brand-title">@Localizer["RightPanelTitle"]</h2>
            <p class="login-brand-subtitle">@Localizer["RightPanelSubtitle"]</p>

            <p class="login-brand-intro">@Localizer["RightPanelIntro"]</p>

            @* Feature list — 4 items, no gold-dot bullets (per user
               directive: "the two texts that existed before don't need
               the dot in front of the sentences"). Plain <p> tags
               instead of <ul><li> with bullet spans. The 2 new texts
               (RightPanelFeature5 + 6) are appended after the
               existing 2. *@
            <div class="login-brand-features">
                <p class="login-brand-feature">@Localizer["RightPanelFeature3"]</p>
                <p class="login-brand-feature">@Localizer["RightPanelFeature4"]</p>
                <p class="login-brand-feature">@Localizer["RightPanelFeature5"]</p>
                <p class="login-brand-feature">@Localizer["RightPanelFeature6"]</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <footer class="login-brand-footer">
            <span>@string.Format(Localizer["RightPanelCopyright"], CultureFormat.FormatDigits(DateTime.UtcNow.Year))</span>
        </footer>
    </aside>
</div>

@* ── Inline culture-switch script ──
   Sets the `takone_culture` cookie in the format ASP.NET Core's
   CookieRequestCultureProvider expects (`c=XX|uic=XX`), then force-reloads
   so the localization middleware re-evaluates on the next request.

   WHY INLINE (not a separate .js file):
     - The function is 3 lines of trivial cookie + reload — too small to
       justify a separate file, an extra HTTP request, a registration in
       App.razor, and a cache-bust version parameter.
     - It's layout-local: only the LoginLayout uses it (the main app's
       MainLayout has its own SwitchCultureAsync via JSRuntime because
       the main app DOES have a Blazor circuit).
     - It MUST run without a Blazor circuit (LoginLayout is static-SSR),
       so it can't use JSRuntime — inline browser JS is the only option.

   Identical in spirit to MainLayout.SwitchCultureAsync — kept duplicated
   because the two layouts live in different worlds (MainLayout always
   has a circuit; LoginLayout never does). *@
<script>
    function takSwitchCulture(culture) {
        var cookieValue = 'c=' + culture + '|uic=' + culture;
        document.cookie = 'takone_culture=' + cookieValue + '; path=/; max-age=31536000; samesite=strict';
        window.location.reload();
    }
</script>
